<p>In November 2025 the Kubernetes project announced that the community Ingress NGINX controller would be retired. Best-effort maintenance continued until March 2026; after that, no further releases, bug fixes or security updates. Existing deployments keep running and the artefacts stay available<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. In January 2026 the Steering and Security Response Committees put it more bluntly: staying on Ingress NGINX after retirement leaves you and your users vulnerable to attack<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>It is now October. If a cluster you are responsible for still runs it, the internet-facing entry point to your platform is a component nobody is patching. That makes this a risk item for the board as much as an engineering task.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What is and isn't changing</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Retired:</strong> the community <code>kubernetes/ingress-nginx</code> controller. Commercial NGINX products from F5 are separate projects<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Not removed:</strong> the Ingress API itself. Google's open source team notes it remains available with no immediate plans for removal, but it is feature-frozen<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Recommended:</strong> Gateway API, which the Kubernetes project calls the modern replacement for Ingress<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</li>
</ul>
<p>So you have two legitimate paths: swap to another Ingress controller, or move to Gateway API. For anything you expect to run for more than a year or two, I recommend Gateway API. Swapping controllers still means translating annotations, so you pay most of the migration cost without the long-term benefit.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why Gateway API is the better destination</h2>
<p>Ingress put everything in one resource and pushed anything beyond host and path routing into controller-specific annotations. Gateway API splits responsibilities across roles: infrastructure providers define a <code>GatewayClass</code>, platform teams run <code>Gateway</code> listeners, and application teams attach <code>HTTPRoute</code> resources<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/ingress-nginx-to-gateway-api/gateway-kind-relationships.svg" alt="Diagram showing a GatewayClass referenced by a Gateway, which an HTTPRoute attaches to, and the HTTPRoute pointing at a Service" width="466" height="130" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>How Gateway API resources relate: GatewayClass, Gateway, HTTPRoute and the backing Service. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/gateway/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes documentation, Gateway API</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>That split matters in organisations. Application teams can change their own routes without touching shared listeners or certificates, and features like header matching, traffic splitting and cross-namespace references are part of the API rather than annotation conventions.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The playbook</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Find every instance.</strong> List clusters running the community controller, including forgotten ones in staging, CI and edge sites. Include Helm charts and vendor products that bundle it.</li>
<li><strong>Inventory annotations.</strong> Export every Ingress object and count the annotations in use. This is where the real effort lives; the WZ-IT migration write-up describes how most behaviour beyond simple routing was expressed through annotations<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Sort them into three buckets: covered by core Gateway API, covered by your chosen implementation's policies, and not supported.</li>
<li><strong>Pick an implementation.</strong> Choose a Gateway API implementation that covers your bucket-two needs, and check its conformance and support model. There is no drop-in replacement, as the Kubernetes statement says<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>, so this choice decides how much translation you do.</li>
<li><strong>Convert, then review by hand.</strong> Use a conversion tool such as ingress2gateway to generate a first draft of Gateway and HTTPRoute resources, then review each one. Generated configuration is a starting point, not a migration.</li>
<li><strong>Run both in parallel.</strong> Stand up the new Gateway alongside the old controller with its own load balancer, and test with real traffic patterns: TLS, redirects, timeouts, body sizes, WebSockets, rate limits.</li>
<li><strong>Shift traffic gradually.</strong> Move hostnames one at a time using DNS weighting or a front-end load balancer, with a tested rollback.</li>
<li><strong>Remove the old controller</strong> and its RBAC, webhooks and CRDs, and add a policy check so it can't come back through a chart dependency.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p>Budget most of the time for step 2. The YAML conversion is quick. Discovering that a critical service depends on an obscure annotation is what turns a two-week migration into a two-month one.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where teams get caught out</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Custom snippets</td><td>Configuration injected as raw NGINX snippets has no equivalent and needs redesign</td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td>External auth annotations need an implementation-specific policy or a move into the application or a gateway layer</td></tr>
<tr><td>TLS</td><td>Certificate references, redirects and minimum TLS versions move to Gateway listeners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Observability</td><td>Access log formats and metrics change; update dashboards and alerts before cutover, using the approach in <a href="/insights/llm-observability-opentelemetry-genai/">LLM observability with OpenTelemetry</a> where relevant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Infrastructure as code</td><td>Update modules and pipelines, as in <a href="/insights/terraform-multi-cloud-module-design/">Terraform module design</a>, so new services don't keep creating Ingress objects</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>A note for AI platforms</h2>
<p>If you serve models on Kubernetes, Gateway API is also where inference-aware routing is heading, with the Gateway API Inference Extension designed for model serving traffic<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. Moving now puts your entry point on the same foundation, which pairs well with the GPU scheduling changes in <a href="/insights/kubernetes-dra-gpu-scheduling/">Kubernetes DRA for GPUs</a>.</p>
</section>

